  "preview": "A school librarian predicts the Spring Book Fair will sell 480 books in 8 days. After 3 days, only 150 books have sold. If the pace holds, how far off will her prediction end up?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use proportional reasoning to extrapolate from a partial rate to a full-period total, and compute percent error as |actual - predicted| / predicted. Distinguish percent error from percent change and from absolute error.",
    "studentPrompt": "Ms. Alvarez runs the Spring Book Fair at Willow Creek Middle School. Before the fair started, she predicted the school would sell 480 books over the 8-day fair.\n\nAfter the first 3 days, the cash register has rung up 150 books.\n\n1. If the rest of the fair continues at the same pace as these first 3 days, how many books will be sold in total by the end of day 8?\n2. Would Ms. Alvarez's prediction end up being too high or too low? By how many books?\n3. What is the percent error of her original prediction? (Percent error = |actual - predicted| / predicted, written as a percent.)\n4. Ms. Alvarez says, \"Don't worry — being off by 5% would still be a great prediction.\" Is her prediction inside that 5% band? Justify with numbers.",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A simple book-fair scene: a row of three colorful books standing on a shelf next to a small sign that reads 'Book Fair,' with a calendar grid showing 8 days where the first 3 are shaded.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#fbf7ef\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"150\" width=\"210\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#a0785a\"/><g><rect x=\"45\" y=\"95\" width=\"22\" height=\"55\" fill=\"#c0504d\" stroke=\"#6b2b2a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"105\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#fbe3a2\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"112\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#fbe3a2\"/></g><g><rect x=\"72\" y=\"85\" width=\"22\" height=\"65\" fill=\"#4f81bd\" stroke=\"#2a4a6b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><rect x=\"76\" y=\"96\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#e8eef7\"/><rect x=\"76\" y=\"103\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#e8eef7\"/></g><g><rect x=\"99\" y=\"100\" width=\"22\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#9bbb59\" stroke=\"#4f6b2a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><rect x=\"103\" y=\"110\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#eef5dd\"/><rect x=\"103\" y=\"117\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#eef5dd\"/></g><g><rect x=\"126\" y=\"90\" width=\"22\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#e8a33d\" stroke=\"#7a5418\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><rect x=\"130\" y=\"101\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#fff3d6\"/><rect x=\"130\" y=\"108\" width=\"14\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#fff3d6\"/></g><g><rect x=\"153\" y=\"110\" width=\"22\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#8064a2\" stroke=\"#3f2e58\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/></g><g><rect x=\"180\" y=\"98\" width=\"22\" height=\"52\" fill=\"#4bacc6\" stroke=\"#214a58\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(155,40)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"90\" height=\"32\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff3d6\" stroke=\"#7a5418\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"45\" y=\"21\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#5a3a14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">Book Fair</text><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"32\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#7a5418\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(300,55)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"150\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#3a6b8a\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">8-Day Fair</text><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2a2a2a\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"30\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#cfe6f2\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"21\" y=\"50\" font-weight=\"700\">1</text><rect x=\"42\" y=\"30\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#cfe6f2\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"58\" y=\"50\" font-weight=\"700\">2</text><rect x=\"79\" y=\"30\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#cfe6f2\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"50\" font-weight=\"700\">3</text><rect x=\"116\" y=\"30\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"132\" y=\"50\">4</text><rect x=\"5\" y=\"66\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"21\" y=\"86\">5</text><rect x=\"42\" y=\"66\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"58\" y=\"86\">6</text><rect x=\"79\" y=\"66\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"86\">7</text><rect x=\"116\" y=\"66\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"132\" y=\"86\">8</text></g></g><text x=\"240\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6a6a6a\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">Shaded days = days completed so far</text></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the calendar. Tell the story in plain words: 'Ms. Alvarez guessed 480 books. After 3 days they've sold 150. Is she on track?' Resist the urge to explain percent error yet — let groups invent the question first. Send them to boards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: calculator (after groups commit to an estimate)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "percent",
      "percent-error",
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "extrapolation",
      "rates"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC task. The first question is a unit-rate problem any group can attack (150 books / 3 days = 50/day). The percent-error framing in Q3 is the diamond — most groups will reach for percent change first and need to debate which denominator is right. Q4 forces them to interpret their number, not just compute it.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Most groups compute 150 / 3 = 50 books/day, then 50 * 8 = 400 books total. A few will scale: 150 books in 3/8 of the fair, so 150 * 8/3 = 400.\n- Q2: 400 actual vs. 480 predicted -> prediction is too high by 80 books.\n- Q3: Percent error = |400 - 480| / 480 = 80/480 = 1/6 ≈ 16.67%. Some groups will divide by 400 (the actual) and get 20% — good moment for class debate about which denominator the formula uses.\n- Q4: 16.67% is well outside a 5% band. A 5% band around 480 is 456 to 504, and 400 is far below that. Her prediction is NOT inside the 5% band.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Computing 'percent of fair completed' (3/8 = 37.5%) and confusing it with percent error.\n- Using actual as the denominator instead of predicted (giving 20% instead of 16.67%) — common because students learned percent change with 'original' as denominator and conflate the two.\n- Stopping at 'off by 80 books' without converting to percent, missing that the question asks for percent error specifically.\n- Forgetting absolute value and reporting -16.67% (the sign doesn't carry in standard percent error).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups got 16.67% and 20%. Which denominator does the percent-error formula use, and why does that choice matter when we're judging a prediction?\n- If the pace picks up in the last 5 days, the percent error could shrink. What daily pace for days 4-8 would land the actual right on 480?\n- 'Off by 80 books' and '16.67% error' describe the same gap. When is the raw-number version more useful, and when is the percent version more useful?",
    "extensions": "- The principal predicts 600 books for next year's fair. The fair ends with 540 books sold. Did the principal do better or worse than Ms. Alvarez? Compare percent errors, not raw differences.\n- Ms. Alvarez wants her prediction to land within 5% no matter what. After 3 days at 150 books, what's the smallest daily rate needed across days 4-8 to bring the percent error down to exactly 5%?\n- Reverse the problem: the fair ended up selling 525 books and Ms. Alvarez's prediction had a 12.5% error. What were the two possible predictions she could have made?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Rate = 150 books / 3 days = 50 books/day. Projected total = 50 * 8 = 400 books.\n2. Predicted 480, projected actual 400. Prediction is too high by 480 - 400 = 80 books.\n3. Percent error = |400 - 480| / 480 = 80/480 = 1/6 ≈ 0.1667 = 16.67%.\n4. A 5% band around 480 is 480 * 0.95 = 456 to 480 * 1.05 = 504. The projected 400 is well below 456, so the prediction is NOT inside the 5% band. The actual percent error (about 16.7%) is more than three times the 5% threshold.",
